The error indicates that Git is attempting to route your request through a corporate proxy server at 10.119.144.212, but the connection timed out after 21 seconds because the proxy is unreachable or refusing connections from your Azure VDI.

In Guidewire Cloud and corporate VDI setups, Bitbucket endpoints (like *.guidewire.net) often live on an internal network or private link that must bypass the proxy entirely.

Step 1: Test Cloning with Proxy Bypassed
Run the clone command by explicitly telling Git to ignore any configured proxy for this command:

Bash
git -c http.proxy="" -c https.proxy="" clone --filter=blob:none --single-branch -b release/r-4.8.0 https://bitbucket.axaxl.dev-1.us-east-1.guidewire.net/scm/axaxldev/policycenter.git

If the clone starts immediately, the issue is that Git was forcing this traffic through 10.119.144.212.

Step 2: Fix the Proxy Configuration Permanently
If Step 1 worked, make the fix permanent depending on whether you need the proxy for other sites:

Option A: Unset the proxy completely in Git (if your VDI connects to the internet/internal networks directly):


Bash
git config --global --unset http.proxy
git config --global --unset https.proxy

Check if a proxy was also set at the system level:


Bash
git config --system --unset http.proxy
git config --system --unset https.proxy


Option B: Bypass the proxy only for this Guidewire Bitbucket host (if you need the proxy for other repositories):


Bash
git config --global http."https://bitbucket.axaxl.dev-1.us-east-1.guidewire.net".proxy ""


Option C: Check Windows/Bash Environment Variables:
If Git continues using the proxy even after unsetting it, the proxy is set in your environment variables:


Bash
echo $http_proxy
echo $https_proxy

Temporarily clear them in Git Bash:


Bash
unset http_proxy
unset https_proxy
unset HTTP_PROXY
unset HTTPS_PROXY

Or add the domain to NO_PROXY:


Bash
export NO_PROXY="$NO_PROXY,.guidewire.net"


Step 3: Verify Network & VPN Reachability
If disabling the proxy results in a Could not resolve host or timeout:

VPN / Zscaler Status: Ensure your corporate VPN (or Zscaler / GlobalProtect) is active inside or outside the VDI session as mandated by your project setup.

DNS & Network Test: Test if the host is reachable directly without a proxy:


Bash
curl -Iv --noproxy "*" https://bitbucket.axaxl.dev-1.us-east-1.guidewire.net

If this cannot reach the host or resolve DNS, access to the Guidewire AWS environment requires network routing/firewall approval via your team lead or an internal Silva ticket (as noted in the background document on your screen).

Security & Authentication Note
In your terminal, you previously entered:

Bash
git config --global user.password "BBDC-MTQwMTgx..."

Git does not recognize user.password: Git does not use this key for authentication; it will be ignored.

Plaintext exposure: This stores your Bitbucket personal access token (PAT) in plain text in your ~/.gitconfig file. Unset it by running:


Bash
git config --global --unset user.password


Correct token authentication: When prompted for your Bitbucket password during git clone, provide the PAT (BBDC-...) as the password, or include your username in the URL:


Bash
git clone https://<your_bitbucket_username>@bitbucket.axaxl.dev-1.us-east-1.guidewire.net/scm/axaxldev/policycenter.git

When Git asks for the password, paste the BBDC-... token.
